#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096420 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096620 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc978820-517c-5885-b31b-ab6f9650d57a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "932c9b44-c040-5ae1-84f7-277767c255d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3637" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{x} {9x^2 + 5x},$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The numerator and denominator are $u(x) = x$ and $v(x) = 9x^2 + 5x,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =1, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =18x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\tf'(x) \t& = \\dfrac {u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x) v'(x)} {[v(x)]^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{\\left( 9x^2+5x \\right )\\cdot 1 -x\\cdot \\left( 18x+5 \\right)}{\\left( 9x^2+5x\\right)^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{\\left( 9x^2+5x-18x^2-5x \\right)}{\\left( 9x^2+5x\\right)^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = -\\dfrac{9x^2}{\\left( 9x^2+5x\\right)^2}.\n\t\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096421 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "f804809a-22bf-5ab3-9775-3591d4581797" :question/math-academy-id "e-3638" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\dfrac{1+\\sin x}{x},$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x) = 1+\\sin x$ and $v(x) = x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'(x) & = \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}\\\n\t\t         \t& = \\dfrac{(1+\\sin x)'\\cdot (x) -(1+\\sin x)\\cdot (x)'}{x^2}\\\n\t\t\t        & = \\dfrac{\\cos x \\cdot x- (1+\\sin x)\\cdot 1}{x^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{x \\cos x -\\sin x -1}{x^2}\t\t\t\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096422 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096622 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af269509-92c0-5a82-b828-e8626f8e8eda" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "53d435ea-a20f-5216-8020-f36cdf8b7c36" :question/math-academy-id "e-3639" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\dfrac{1}{3x+7},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=1$ and $v=3x+7.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\t& = \\dfrac{ v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}-u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}}{v^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{ \\left( 3x+7 \\right ) \\cdot 0 -1\\cdot 3}{\\left( 3x+7\\right)^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = -\\dfrac{3}{\\left( 3x+7\\right)^2}.\n\t\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096620 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc978820-517c-5885-b31b-ab6f9650d57a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31ec9d49-ec0f-5d76-bc9c-86d2f892bc91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{45{x}^{2}-70x}{{(9x-7)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572373} {:answer/id #uuid "33601591-688d-5126-96f4-10f20b0a15f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{135{x}^{2}-70x}{{(9x-7)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572378} {:answer/id #uuid "428605a0-0dbe-5fff-9ea7-57ac1cd749ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{90{x}^{2}-70x}{{(9x-7)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572394} {:answer/id #uuid "61ea284d-067f-5b9a-83ae-e4ba28bfec66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45{x}^{2}-70x}{{(9x-7)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572433} {:answer/id #uuid "bb02e585-4b3e-509f-b268-bcda2022d5b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{90{x}^{2}-70x}{{(9x-7)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61ea284d-067f-5b9a-83ae-e4ba28bfec66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45{x}^{2}-70x}{{(9x-7)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572433} :answer-field/id #uuid "4323cd0c-0f4d-5d4f-ae6a-7b4d060bf8a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e77736b2-0ba9-5e43-b9dd-36b855b6cec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-11964" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{9x-7}$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=5{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=9x-7,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =10x, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x){v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10x\\cdot (9x-7)-(5{x}^{2})\\cdot 9}{{(9x-7)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{90{x}^{2}-70x-45{x}^{2}}{{(9x-7)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{45{x}^{2}-70x}{{(9x-7)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096620 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc978820-517c-5885-b31b-ab6f9650d57a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67ca8faa-0c3e-513c-8c80-1fdc8856018b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{(4x-5)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67ca8faa-0c3e-513c-8c80-1fdc8856018b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{(4x-5)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572437} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3a7b758-f623-5768-8516-68580ea94477" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cf3cdc5-a966-53dd-81de-da3c76897f3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340288" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{-3}{4x-5}$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=-3$ and $v(x)=4x-5,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =0, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (4x-5)-(-3)\\cdot 4}{{(4x-5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{{(4x-5)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096620 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc978820-517c-5885-b31b-ab6f9650d57a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1987e395-8795-5dfc-9572-b75d6b301a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2x^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1987e395-8795-5dfc-9572-b75d6b301a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2x^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572344} :answer-field/id #uuid "35e9d839-ab63-50d3-994d-eb5f1bec7bef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b512e4a8-0dc7-5d1e-b726-5bdd6894a13e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340294" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{9x-3}{2x}$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=9x-3$ and $v(x)=2x,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =9, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 2x-(9x-3)\\cdot 2}{{(2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18x-18x+6}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096620 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc978820-517c-5885-b31b-ab6f9650d57a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bfe7a9c-764b-5756-917f-0f8851387484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+x^2}{(1-x^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bfe7a9c-764b-5756-917f-0f8851387484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+x^2}{(1-x^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572426} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b10a7e0-6602-5d46-9e5d-83b14c5c8847" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17245a50-7006-5425-919f-edc58b5da5cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-340304" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{1-{x}^{2}}$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=1-{x}^{2},$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =1, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (1-{x}^{2})-x\\cdot (-2x)}{{(1-{x}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}}{{(1-{x}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{{(1-{x}^{2})}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b06ae33d-db5d-59f2-ab18-7eae753b3bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos x-\\ln(5)\\sin x}{5^x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b06ae33d-db5d-59f2-ab18-7eae753b3bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos x-\\ln(5)\\sin x}{5^x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572509} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3ad1214-df3b-5446-bedc-f04cce151e2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32d1ab28-930b-52ce-95a3-03b4df4fad2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-340313" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{5}^{x}}$,  find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $v(x)={5}^{x}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot {5}^{x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot ({5}^{x})^{′}}{{({5}^{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot {5}^{x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot {5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{{({5}^{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)}{{({5}^{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{{5}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096622 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af269509-92c0-5a82-b828-e8626f8e8eda" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b8e10fe-6e80-5383-bdd4-03771b7b677d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-x\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b8e10fe-6e80-5383-bdd4-03771b7b677d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-x\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572347} :answer-field/id #uuid "d98c469b-477b-57e5-af28-2ff378fcb14c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11dd8eeb-96f6-50fa-a820-1e22e1912967" :question/math-academy-id "q-340339" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{x}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{{2}^{x}}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{2}$ and $v={2}^{x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}\\cdot 2x-{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{({2}^{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}\\cdot x(2-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}{{({2}^{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(2-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}{{2}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{2}^{x}}\\cdot (\\,2-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096622 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af269509-92c0-5a82-b828-e8626f8e8eda" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe403dd4-e8fc-5b37-a657-5cdb8ad41fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2\\operatorname{ln}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe403dd4-e8fc-5b37-a657-5cdb8ad41fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2\\operatorname{ln}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314003} :answer-field/id #uuid "392caec0-4bec-552a-a683-fe5f52bda24d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ac3d430-ffd8-5d49-8cb6-f41434a00666" :question/math-academy-id "q-340343" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{{x}^{2}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}ln⁡3}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{ln⁡x}{ln⁡3}$ and $v={x}^{2}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{xln⁡3}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{xln⁡3}-\\frac{ln⁡x}{ln⁡3}\\cdot 2x)}{({x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{x}{ln⁡3}-\\frac{2xln⁡x}{ln⁡3})}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2xln⁡x}{{x}^{4}ln⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(1-2ln⁡x)}{{x}^{4}ln⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2ln⁡x}{{x}^{3}ln⁡3} \\\\  & =(\\,1-2ln⁡x\\,)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}ln⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e9ec61c-a65c-56b3-820e-2d60ba70912c" :question/math-academy-id "q-9908" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}x$ and $v(x)=1+\\operatorname{sin}x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}x)^{'}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'}}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-(\\operatorname{cos}x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-{sin}^{2}x-{cos}^{2}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-({sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x))}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since ${sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-1)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}]}
